    content: 'What is the passage mainly about?',
    passage: 'The fast fashion industry, characterized by rapidly produced, low-cost clothing that mirrors the latest runway trends, has transformed the global apparel market over the past two decades. Companies like Zara, H&M, and Shein can design, manufacture, and distribute new styles in a matter of weeks, a process that once took months. This speed allows consumers to refresh their wardrobes frequently at affordable prices. However, the environmental and social costs of fast fashion are increasingly difficult to ignore. The industry is responsible for approximately ten percent of global carbon emissions—more than international flights and maritime shipping combined. Water consumption is equally staggering: producing a single cotton T-shirt requires about 2,700 liters of water, enough for one person to drink for two and a half years. Furthermore, the rise of "disposable fashion" has led to enormous textile waste. In the United States alone, the average person discards roughly 37 kilograms of clothing each year, most of which ends up in landfills or incinerators. Labor practices in fast fashion supply chains have also drawn criticism. Investigations have revealed unsafe working conditions, child labor, and wages below the poverty line in factories across South Asia and Southeast Asia. In response, a growing movement toward sustainable fashion has emerged. Some brands are adopting recycled materials, implementing take-back programs, and committing to transparency in their supply chains. Consumers, too, are becoming more conscious, with surveys showing that over sixty percent of millennials prefer to buy from brands that demonstrate environmental and social responsibility.',
