    passage: 'Sleep is not merely a passive state of rest; it is an active and essential process for learning and memory. Neuroscientists have discovered that during sleep, the brain replays and consolidates experiences from the waking hours, transferring information from short-term to long-term storage. This process, known as memory consolidation, occurs primarily during two stages of sleep: slow-wave sleep (SWS) and rapid eye movement (REM) sleep. Slow-wave sleep, which dominates the first half of the night, is particularly important for declarative memory—the type of memory responsible for facts and events. Studies have shown that students who sleep for at least seven hours after studying retain significantly more information than those who stay up late cramming. REM sleep, which becomes more prevalent in the second half of the night, plays a critical role in procedural memory—the kind of memory involved in skills and motor tasks, such as playing a musical instrument or riding a bicycle. The implications for education are profound. Schools that have shifted their start times later—allowing teenagers to get more complete sleep cycles—have reported improvements in academic performance, attendance, and mental health. Yet many educational systems still prioritize instructional hours over sleep, inadvertently undermining the very learning they seek to promote. Researchers advocate for a paradigm shift: treating sleep not as lost time but as a vital component of the learning process itself.',
